3. Key OOP Concepts in Job 22
A. The super() Function
 * Purpose: Calls methods from the parent class inside the child class.
 * Why super().__init__(tag) Must Come First: The foundational base parts of the machine (tag, running status, hours) must exist before adding specialized components. Skipping this line leaves the child machine missing its basic framework.
B. Method Overriding
 * When a child class defines a method with the exact same name as its parent (e.g., status()), Python executes the child's version instead.
 * A child can extend parent behavior by combining super().status() with extra child-specific data.
4. The "IS-A" Test for Inheritance
To verify if inheritance is appropriate, use the "IS-A" test:
 * Valid Inheritance ("IS-A"): "A Lathe IS A Machine" \rightarrow class Lathe(Machine):
 * Invalid Inheritance ("HAS-A"): "A Lathe HAS A chuck" \rightarrow A chuck is an attribute, not a parent class. Connecting classes that fail this test creates incorrect family structures.
